39-                                                                                     1
40-1    Introduction
41-
42-In 1988 E. Witten [55] proposed new invariants ZkG (X, L) ∈ C of an arbitrary 3–
43-manifold X with an embedded colored link L by quantizing the Chern–Simons
44-field theory associated to a simple and simply connected compact Lie group
45-G, k being an arbitrary positive integer called the (quantum) level. (Here and
46-in the following a 3–manifold is a closed oriented 3–manifold. In particular
47-a Seifert manifold is an oriented Seifert manifold.) The invariant ZkG (X, L) is
48-given by a Feynman path integral over the (infinite dimensional) space of gauge
49-equivalence classes of connections in a G bundle over X . This integral should
50-be understood in a formal way since, at the moment of writing, it seems that
51-no mathematically rigorous definition is known, cf. [27, Sect. 20.2.A]. We will
52-call the invariants ZkG for the quantum G–invariants or the Witten invariants
53-associated to G.
54-N. Reshetikhin and V. G. Turaev [44] were the first to give a rigorous construc-
55-tion of quantum invariants of 3–manifolds with embedded knots. In fact they
56-                           sl (C)
57-constructed invariants τr 2 (X, L) ∈ C of the pair (X, L) by combinatorial
58-means using surgery presentations of (X, L) and irreducible representations of
59-the quantum deformations of sl2 (C) at certain roots of unity, r being an inte-
60-ger ≥ 2 associated to the order of the root of unity. Later quantum invariants
61-τrg(X, L) ∈ C associated to other complex simple Lie algebras g were con-
62-structed using representations of the quantum deformations of g at ‘nice’ roots
63-of unity, see [53]. We call τrg for the quantum g–invariants or the RT–invariants
64-associated to g.
65-Both in Witten’s approach and in the approach of Reshetikhin and Turaev the
66-invariants are part of a family of topological quantum field theories (TQFT’s).
67-This implies that the invariants are defined for compact oriented 3–dimensional
68-cobordisms (perhaps with some extra structure on the boundary), and sat-
69-isfy certain cut-and-paste axioms, see [4], [11], [42], [52]. The TQFT’s of
70-Reshetikhin and Turaev can from an algebraic point of view be given a more
71-general formulation by using so-called modular (tensor) categories [52]. The
72-representation theory of the quantum deformations of g at certain roots of
73-unity, g an arbitrary finite dimensional complex simple Lie algebra, induces
74-such modular categories, see e.g. [32], [9], [35].
75-The invariants of Witten are defined by means of a path integral as stated
76-above. A natural way in physics to obtain information about quantities defined
77-by means of such path integrals is to study their perturbative (or asymptotic)
78-expansion for large level k . In fact, by using stationary phase approximation
79-
80-                                       2
81-techniques together with path integral arguments Witten was able [55] to ex-
82-press the leading large k asymptotics (or the so-called semiclassical approxima-
83-tion) of ZkG (X) as a sum over the set of stationary points for the Chern–Simons
84-functional. The terms in this sum are expressed by such topological/geometric
85-invariants as Chern–Simons invariants, Reidemeister torsions and spectral flows,
86-so here we see a way to extract topological information from the invariants. A
87-full asymptotic expansion of Witten’s invariant is expected on the basis of a
88-full perturbative analysis of the Feynman path integral, see [7], [8], [6].
89-The first rigorous verifications of the conjectured formula for the semiclassical
90-approximation were given, partly by Freed and Gompf [13] presenting a large
91:amount of computer calculations for the SU (2)–invariants of lens spaces and
92-some 3–fibered Seifeirt manifolds, and about the same time by Jeffrey [26] and
93-Garoufalidis [14] who independently gave exact calculations of the semiclassical
94:approximation of the SU (2)–invariants of lens spaces. Jeffrey also verified parts
95-of the conjecture for the semiclassical approximation of ZkG (X) for G arbitrary
96-and X belonging to a class of mapping tori of the torus.
97-It is generally believed that the family of TQFT’s of Reshetikhin and Turaev is a
98-mathematical realization of Witten family of TQFT’s. This belief has together
99-with the above works on the perturbative expansion of Witten’s invariants led
100-to a detailed conjecture, the asymptotic expansion conjecture (AEC), which
101-specifies the asymptotic behaviour of the RT–invariants. The AEC was pro-
102-posed by Andersen in [1], where he proved it for mapping tori of finite order
103-diffeomorphisms of orientable surfaces of genus at least two using the gauge
104-theoretic approach to the quantum invariants.
105-Let us give an outline of the results obtained in this paper. Firstly, we deter-
106-mine formulas for the invariants τrg of all Seifert manifolds in terms of the Seifert
107-invariants and standard data for g, g being an arbitrary complex finite dimen-
108-sional simple Lie algebra, cf. Theorem 4.3. Theorem 4.3 is a generalization of
109-[17, Theorem 8.4]. For a certain subclass of the Seifert manifolds, containing all
110-the Seifert fibered integral homology spheres, we simplify the expression for the
111-invariants considerable, cf. Theorem 4.8. This result is a generalization of [33,
112-Formula (4.2)]. Secondly, we analyse more carefully the invariants τrg(X) for X
113:any lens space. Theorem 5.1 gives the result for any lens space and any of the
114:invariants τrg . Proposition 5.2 gives more compact expressions for τrg(L(p, q))
115:in case r and p are coprime. A main corollary of Theorem 5.1, Corollary 5.5,
116-is a determination of the large r asymptotics of the quantum g–invariants of
117:the lens spaces. The result is in agreement with the AEC, and leads together
118-with the AEC to a Conjecture 5.6 for the Chern–Simons invariants of the flat G
119:connections on any lens space, G being an arbitrary simply connected, compact
120-
121-                                         3
122:simple Lie group. All the results for lens spaces are generalizations of results
123:in [26], which considered the g = sl2 (C) case. To be precise Theorem 5.1 gen-
124-eralizes [26, Theorem 3.4], Proposition 5.2 generalizes [26, Theorem 3.7], and
125-Corollary 5.5 is a generalization of [26, Formula (5.7)].
126-We have via recent private communication learned that J. E. Andersen has
127-for the groups G = SU (n) proved the asymptotic expansion conjecture for
128-all closed 3–manifolds via the gauge theoretic approach. The proof involves
129-asymptotics of Hitchin’s connection over Teichmüller space, approximations to
130-all orders, of the boundary states of handle bodies and techniques similar to
131-the ones presented in [3]. Where Andersen works with the gauge theoretic
132-definition of the quantum invariant, we work with the definition of Reshetikhin
133:and Turaev and our proof of the AEC for lens spaces is very different from
134-Andersen’s general proof in the SU (n)–case.
135-A major part of the paper is concerned with studying a certain family of finite
136-dimensional complex representations Rgr of SL(2, Z), one representation for
137-each pair g, r. These representations are known from the study of theta func-
138-tions and modular forms in connection with the study of affine Lie algebras, cf.
139-[29], [28, Sect. 13]. They also play a fundamental role in conformal field theory
140-and (therefore) in the Chern–Simons TQFT’s of Witten, see e.g. [15], [54], [55].
141-In case g = sl2 (C), Jeffrey [25], [26] has determined a nice formula for Rgr (U )
142-in terms of the entries in U ∈ SL(2, Z) and the integer r. Theorem 2.6 is a
143-generalization of Jeffrey’s result to arbitrary g, compare with [26, Sect. 2]. The
144-representations Rgr are of interest when calculating the RT–invariants of the
145-Seifert manifolds since certain matrices, which can be expressed through these
146-representations, enter into the formulas of the invariants.
147-The paper is organized as follows. In Sect. 2 we derive formulas for the repre-
148-sentations Rgr . Sect. 3 is a short section intended to introduce notation for the
149-Seifert manifolds. Moreover, we recall surgery presentations for these manifolds
150-due to Montesinos [39]. In Sect. 4 we recall the formulas for the RT–invariants
151-of the Seifert manifolds for an arbitrary modular category. These formulas
152-are then used together with the results in Sect. 2 to calculate the g–invariants
153:of the Seifert manifolds. In Sect. 5 we analyse the case of lens spaces more
154-carefully. In the final Sect 6 we state a rational surgery formula for the quan-
155-tum g–invariants, specializing a rational surgery formula [17, Theorem 5.3] for
156-the RT–invariants associated to an arbitrary modular category. Besides an ap-
157-pendix is added presenting two related proofs of a reciprocity formula for Gauss
158-sums, Proposition 2.2, which plays a vital role in this paper.
159-After having finished this work, we learned via private communication that
160-Marino has obtained a similar result as ours Theorem 4.8 in case the Siefert
161-
162-                                        4
163-manifold has base S 2 and the Lie algebra g is simply laced, cf. [38, Formula
164-(4.11)]. It seems that the result Theorem 2.6 (in the form of the first formula in
165-Corollary 2.7) has been known in the mathematical physics literature for some
166-time at least for the simply laced case, cf. [38, Formula (2.5)], [45, Formula
167-(1.6)]. We have, however, not been able to find any proof of this result in the
168-literature.
169-This paper is an extensive expansion of the paper [19] and gives also the details
170-left out in that paper, in particular the proof of the main Lemma 2.4.
