1386-3     Seifert manifolds
1387-
1388-For Seifert manifolds we will use the notation introduced by Seifert in his
1389-classification results for these manifolds, see [49], [50], [17, Sect. 2]. That
1390-is, (ǫ; g | b; (α1 , β1 ), . . . , (αn , βn )) is the Seifert manifold with orientable base
1391-of genus g ≥ 0 if ǫ = o and non-orientable base of genus g > 0 if ǫ = n (where
1392-the genus of the non-orientable connected sum #k RP2 is k ). (In [49], [50]
1393-(ǫ; g | b; (α1 , β1 ), . . . , (αn , βn )) is denoted (O, ǫ; g | b; α1 , β1 ; . . . ; αn , βn ), but we
1394-leave out the O since we are only dealing with oriented Seifert manifolds.) The
1395-pair (αj , βj ) of coprime integers is the (oriented) Seifert invariant of the j ’th
1396-exceptional (or singular) fiber. We have 0 < βj < αj . The integer −b is equal
1397-to the Euler number of the Seifert fibration (ǫ; g | b) (which is a locally trivial
1398-S 1 –bundle). The sign is chosen so that the Euler number of the spherical (or
1399-unit) tangent bundle over an orientable surface Σ is equal to the Euler charac-
1400-teristic of Σ, see [39, Chap. 1 and 4], [48, Sect. 3]. More generally                       the Seifert
1401-                                                                                                      
1402-                                                                                        Pn
1403-Euler number of (ǫ; g | b; (α1 , β1 ), . . . , (αn , βn )) is E = − b + j=1 βj /αj .
1404:We note that lens spaces are Seifert manifolds with base S 2 and zero, one
1405-or two exceptional fibers. According to [39, Fig. 12 p. 146], the manifold
1406-(ǫ; g | b; (α1 , β1 ), . . . , (αn , βn )) has a surgery presentation as shown in Fig. 1 if
1407-ǫ = o and as shown in Fig. 2 if ǫ = n. The g indicate g repetitions.
